00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:35,680
 use to weld two materials together. Generally speaking, all of these techniques kind of work

10
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:40,240
 the same way. They involve injecting a huge amount of heat into a specific area where you'd

11
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:44,960
 like two metals to be joined. And by melting both of these parent metals and by adding some kind

12
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:50,240
 of extra film metal, you create an incredibly strong joint by bonding these things together.

13
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:54,800
 Welding is an incredibly robust way of attaching metals because the net result is that the two

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,360
 actually become one. A good weld is said to achieve deep penetration and the net result of this

15
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:03,840
 is that if you cut through a weld, generally speaking you can't tell where one metal ends and the

16
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:08,400
 next begins. There are a few considerations that you need to make when welding components.

17
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,800
 Up first of all, you should be aware that the intense heat is generated when welding rapidly

18
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:17,600
 accelerates corrosion in and around the site of the weld. As a result, most welding processes

19
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,480
 tend to incorporate some sort of shielding gas or flux or other protectant to insulate the

20
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,400
 weld site from the rest of the atmosphere and protect the world from corrosion. In most cases,

21
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,640
 welding without some sort of protection will not only result in rapid corrosion over the weld area

22
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:35,520
 but will also result in a pretty terrible weld. Exposure to oxygen has a tendency to introduce

23
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:40,400
 cracks and porosity and other kinds of crap into your welds. These contaminated welds in

